@*
    Risk Acceptance Approval Authority -- migration 271.

    Screen key : `risk-acceptance-authority`
    Group      : Organization
    Menu       : nav-organization -> Risk Acceptance Approval Authority

    AN ORGANIZATION CONFIGURATION SCREEN, NOT A RISK MODULE ONE.
    It follows org-sla-config's conventions -- pm-page-heading compact,
    an org picker in a pm-toolbar, a pm-panel and a pm-table-wrap grid --
    so it reads as a native Organization page rather than a Risk screen
    that happens to live under a different menu.

    WHAT IT CONFIGURES
    Who may approve ACCEPTING a risk, per rating level, separately for
    the inherent score and the residual one. Those are different
    decisions: accepting a Critical risk as-is is not the same act as
    accepting one whose residual score is Low after treatment, and they
    are routinely different people.

    THE LEVELS ARE NOT A FIXED LIST
    Rows come from this organisation's own risk_matrix_cell. rating_code
    is free text by design (204) and the matrix is per-organisation, so a
    four-band matrix shows four rows and a five-band one shows five, with
    no code change. Hard-coding five would have produced rows the matrix
    can never emit and omitted 'Medium', which it emits constantly.

    IT DOES NOT REPLACE org_risk_config
    212's approver_role_id still applies wherever a level has no row.
    Levels using it are labelled, so an unconfigured row reads as
    "falls back to X" rather than as "nobody approves this".

    Backend:
        GET  /practice/api/risk-centre/acceptance-authority?organizationId=
        POST /practice/api/risk-centre/acceptance-authority
*@
@model PracticeManagement.Web.Models.PracticeScreen

<div class="pm-page-heading compact">
    <div>
        <p class="pm-eyebrow">@Model.Group / @Model.Title</p>
        <h1>@Model.Title</h1>
        <p>
            Who may approve accepting a risk, per rating level &mdash; separately for the
            inherent score and the residual score after treatment.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<section class="pm-panel" id="raaRoot">
    <div class="pm-toolbar" style="flex-wrap:wrap; gap:8px; align-items:end;">
        <label style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; font-size:12px; color:#475569;">
            <span>Organization</span>
            <select id="raaPickOrg"
                    style="padding:6px 8px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius:4px; margin-top:2px; min-width:260px;">
                <option value="">Loading...</option>
            </select>
        </label>
        <div style="display:flex; gap:6px;">
            <button class="pm-button" id="raaReloadBtn" type="button">Reload</button>
        </div>
    </div>

    @* The 212 settings this page defers to. Shown because an
       unconfigured level is not "unapproved" -- it falls back here, and
       hiding that would make the grid look emptier than it is.

       Styled locally as .raa-note rather than reusing Risk Centre's
       .risk-gate: that class is declared inside risk-centre.cshtml's own
       <style> block, which this page never loads, so borrowing the name
       would have rendered an unstyled div. *@
    <div id="raaFallback" class="raa-note" style="display:none;"></div>

    <div id="raaMessage" hidden
         style="margin:8px 0; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:6px; font-size:13px;"></div>

    <div class="pm-table-wrap" style="margin-top:12px;">
        <table id="raaTable">
            <thead>
                @* Widths live in CSS, not inline, so the two authority
                   columns share ONE rule and cannot drift apart. *@
                <tr>
                    <th class="raa-col-level">Risk level</th>
                    <th class="raa-col-auth">Inherent risk &mdash; approval authority</th>
                    <th class="raa-col-same">Residual same as inherent</th>
                    <th class="raa-col-auth">Residual risk &mdash; approval authority</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody id="raaTableBody">
                <tr><td colspan="4" class="pm-empty-row">Select an organization.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="ra-actionbar" style="display:flex; justify-content:space-between; align-items:center; gap:12px; margin-top:12px;">
        <p class="pm-hint" style="margin:0;">
            Leaving a level unset is allowed &mdash; it falls back to the organisation's
            general risk approver. Every change is recorded with its old and new value.
        </p>
        <div style="display:flex; gap:8px;">
            <button type="button" class="pm-button" id="raaResetBtn">Discard changes</button>
            <button type="button" class="pm-button primary" id="raaSaveBtn">
                <i class="fa-solid fa-floppy-disk"></i> Save
            </button>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<style>
    /* The fallback notice. Declared here rather than borrowed from Risk
       Centre's .risk-gate, which lives in that partial's own style block
       and is not loaded on this page. */
    #raaRoot .raa-note {
        padding: 10px 12px; border-radius: 6px; font-size: 12px;
        background: #ebf8ff; border: 1px solid #bee3f8; color: #2a4365;
        margin-bottom: 10px;
    }
    /* No general approver configured: an unset level then has no
       authority at all, which is a warning rather than information. */
    #raaRoot .raa-note.is-blocked {
        background: #fffaf0; border-color: #fbd38d; color: #7b341e;
    }

    /* ---- Column widths -------------------------------------------
       table-layout: fixed is what makes the two authority columns
       PROVABLY equal. Left on auto, the browser sizes each column by
       its content, and the inherent column carries an extra
       "Currently X (organisation default)" hint on some rows -- so the
       two halves of a matrix that is meant to read as a pair drifted
       apart by however long that role name happened to be.

       Fixed layout also means the declared widths are honoured exactly
       rather than treated as suggestions.

       16 + 34 + 16 + 34 = 100. Percentages, not pixels, so the table
       uses the full width it is given at any size. */
    #raaRoot #raaTable {
        width: 100%;
        table-layout: fixed;
        /* Below this the dropdowns start truncating role names; the
           surrounding .pm-table-wrap scrolls instead of crushing them. */
        min-width: 760px;
    }
    #raaRoot .raa-col-level { width: 16%; }
    /* ONE rule for BOTH authority columns -- they cannot disagree. */
    #raaRoot .raa-col-auth  { width: 34%; }
    #raaRoot .raa-col-same  { width: 16%; }

    /* The header sits over a centred checkbox, so it is centred too.
       Previously the cell was centred and its header was not, which
       read as the column being misaligned. */
    #raaRoot .raa-col-same,
    #raaRoot .raa-same-cell { text-align: center; }

    /* Scoped to #raaRoot. The grid is the page, so it gets a little
       breathing room the generic table does not have. */
    #raaRoot #raaTable th { vertical-align: bottom; }
    #raaRoot #raaTable td { vertical-align: top; padding-top: 7px; padding-bottom: 7px; }

    /* width:100% inside equal columns makes the two dropdowns equal by
       construction rather than by matching two numbers by hand. */
    #raaRoot #raaTable select {
        width: 100%; padding: 5px 8px; border: 1px solid #cbd5e1;
        border-radius: 4px; font: inherit; font-size: 13px; background: #fff;
    }
    #raaRoot #raaTable select:disabled { background: #f1f5f9; color: #64748b; }

    /* Fixed layout will not widen a column for a long level name, so
       let it wrap rather than spill. */
    #raaRoot .raa-level { font-weight: 600; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
    /* A level running on the org-wide role rather than its own setting.
       Said in the row, because the dropdown alone cannot show it. */
    #raaRoot .raa-fallback { font-size: 11px; color: #92400e; display: block; margin-top: 2px; }

    /* The checkbox sits between two dropdowns, so it lines up with THEM
       rather than with the top of its cell -- the cells are top-aligned
       because the fallback hint below a dropdown must not push the
       controls out of line with each other. */
    #raaRoot .raa-same-cell input[type="checkbox"] { margin-top: 8px; }

    #raaRoot tr.raa-dirty > td:first-child { box-shadow: inset 3px 0 0 #2b6cb0; }

    /* Responsive. The table keeps its 760px floor and .pm-table-wrap
       scrolls below that, so nothing is ever crushed. On a medium
       window the two label columns give their space to the dropdowns,
       which are the part that actually needs it -- and they give it
       EQUALLY, so the two authority columns stay identical. */
    @@media (max-width: 1200px) {
        #raaRoot .raa-col-level { width: 14%; }
        #raaRoot .raa-col-auth  { width: 36%; }
        #raaRoot .raa-col-same  { width: 14%; }
    }
</style>

<script src="~/js/RiskCentre/risk-acceptance-authority.js" asp-append-version="true"></script>
